First, we will discuss the coordination issues related to the transformation of mechanical systems. As previously mentioned, the key lies in collecting load information when powering mechanical systems. This includes:
- Whether the system is powered by a regular power supply or an emergency power supply, and whether it has redundant configurations;
- The type of heating and cooling systems to be adopted—while this has been discussed, it still needs to be finalized in the final design;
- The power connection method for each piece of equipment, especially packaged units like air conditioning systems, whether they require a single power connection or multiple circuits.

The mechanical section typically provides two types of technical specifications: one is the mechanical equipment list, detailing all equipment technical information, including power requirements; the other is the equipment layout diagram, indicating the specific physical locations of equipment within the project.

The following three points pertain to Louis Kuhn’s requirements:
1. Control schematic diagram, as previously mentioned;
2. Operation sequence;
3. Marking the control center’s location on the drawings—i.e., the installation positions of Louis Kuhn’s computers, servers, and other equipment. Additionally, the location of each DDC controller must be indicated. All these require corresponding power supply provisions from us.

In North America, Louis Kuhn is typically designed by the mechanical section, as is the IT portion.
